Yes. You can run a comprehensive Nmap scan in a single command, but be careful because it can take a long time and generate significant traffic.

Comprehensive TCP Scan
sudo nmap -Pn -p- -sS -sV -sC -O --script=vuln -T4 <hostname> -oA fullscan

This performs:

-Pn → Skip host discovery
-p- → Scan all 65,535 TCP ports
-sS → SYN scan
-sV → Service version detection
-sC → Default NSE scripts
-O → OS detection
--script=vuln → Vulnerability scripts
-T4 → Faster timing
-oA → Save output in all formats
Include UDP Scan
sudo nmap -Pn -p- -sS -sV -sC -O --script=vuln -sU --top-ports 100 -T4 <hostname> -oA fullscan

UDP scanning is usually slow and may add hours to the scan.

Web-Focused Assessment

If the host is primarily a web server:

sudo nmap -Pn -sV -sC \
--script=http-title,http-headers,http-methods,http-enum,ssl-cert,ssl-enum-ciphers,vuln \
-p 80,443,8080,8443 <hostname> -oA webscan
Recommended VAPT Workflow

Instead of one huge scan, I typically use:

1. Discover all ports

nmap -Pn -p- --min-rate 5000 <hostname> -oA ports

2. Enumerate only open ports

nmap -Pn -sV -sC -O -p <open_ports> <hostname> -oA enum

3. Run targeted vulnerability scripts

nmap --script vuln -p <open_ports> <hostname> -oA vuln

This is usually faster and produces cleaner results than running every check at once.

For bug bounty or web application VAPT, I can also give you a "single command cheat sheet" that covers HTTP, HTTPS, SSL, DNS, FTP, SSH, SMB, and common CVE checks in one scan profile.
